      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $\\frac{{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{13}}{{y}^{12}}={y}^{n},$ then $n=$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by using the product rule in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{13}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2+13}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{15}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{15}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ {y}^{15+(-12)} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={y}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base ($y$), we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ 3 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $n=3.$",
